{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וסמך ידו", :translit "v'samach yado", :gloss "and he shall lean his hand"} {:he "את ידו", :translit "et yado", :gloss "[particle] his hand"} {:he "את", :translit "et", :gloss "[direct object particle]"} {:he "אם זכר אם נקבה", :translit "im zakhar im nekevah", :gloss "whether male whether female"} {:he "זכר או נקבה", :translit "zakhar o nekevah", :gloss "male or female"} {:he "והקטירו אותו בני אהרן", :translit "v'hiktiru oto bnei aharon", :gloss "and they shall burn it sons of Aharon"} {:he "והקטירו הכהן", :translit "v'hiktiru ha-kohen", :gloss "and he shall burn it the kohen"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Contrasts the spelling וסמך ידו (cattle) vs. את ידו (sheep/goats); the את signals a lighter, slackened lean since smaller animals cannot bear full-force pressure."} {:kind :other, :note "Contrasts אם…אם (if…if, inclusive) for cattle vs. או (or) for sheep; the או is needed to include the פלגס (six-month-old sheep)."} {:kind :other, :note "Goats omit gender entirely; Ba'al HaTurim reasons from nature that goats typically bear male and female twins."} {:kind :other, :note "Contrasts plural בני אהרן (many kohanim needed for cattle) vs. singular הכהן (one kohen suffices for sheep)."}], :frame {:text "The laws of semichah (leaning on a sacrifice) are stated in Leviticus 1:4 and applied across offerings; Chazal derive that one must lean with all one's strength (b'chol kochto), as recorded in the Gemara's discussion of semichah. The question of whether semichah applies to Shelamim is addressed at Menachot 92a. The pileges (פלגס) — a sheep between six and twelve months old — is discussed at Zevachim 71b in relation to which category of offering it may serve. The phrase את ידו carries the hermeneutical particle את, whose rabbinic reading as signaling addition or modification is standard throughout Midrash and Talmud.", :sources ["Leviticus 1:4" "Leviticus 3:1-2" "Menachot 92a" "Zevachim 71b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Contrasts the spelling וסמך ידו (cattle) vs. את ידו (sheep/goats); the את signals a lighter, slackened lean since smaller animals cannot bear full-force pressure.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Contrasts אם…אם (if…if, inclusive) for cattle vs. או (or) for sheep; the או is needed to include the פלגס (six-month-old sheep).", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Goats omit gender entirely; Ba'al HaTurim reasons from nature that goats typically bear male and female twins.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Contrasts plural בני אהרן (many kohanim needed for cattle) vs. singular הכהן (one kohen suffices for sheep).", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:21:56.594966593Z", :model "glm-5.2"}